Explanation:
Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Statutory & Administrative Authority:
 - Section 41, Chapter 10, Book I of Executive Order No. 292 (Administrative Code of 1987, as amended by RA 6733 & RA 9406) — Officers Authorized to Administer Oaths:
 (a) President, Vice-President, Members of Congress;
 (b) Justices, Judges, and Clerks of Court;
 (c) Department Secretaries, Undersecretaries, Bureau Directors, Regional Directors, Heads of Agencies;
 (d) Provincial Governors, Vice-Governors, City/Municipal Mayors;
 (e) Public Prosecutors;
 (f) Notaries Public;
 (g) Specially designated HR / Admin officers in government agencies authorized by the head of office.
2. In practice in the Philippine Civil Service:
 - Internal Admin/HR officers administer the oath for agency employees FREE OF CHARGE.
 - External Notaries Public or Judges may also administer the oath.
3. Option A is the correct, comprehensive legal list of authorized administering officers.

Common Trap: Believing only paid private lawyers can notarize SALNs; internal agency HR officers and heads of office are STATUTORILY AUTHORIZED to administer SALN oaths for free.
Exam Tip: Administering SALN Oaths = Agency Heads/Designated Admin Officers (Free), Judges, Clerks of Court, Prosecutors, and Notaries Public.
